Build for a target with no fork() and no working atomics

A NOMMU system cannot have fork(): without address translation the copy shares
the parent's memory, so every pointer the child holds still points into the
parent. uClibc does not provide the symbol on such targets, and referring to it
is a link error -- or worse, a shared library that loads happily and falls over
much later, a long way from the cause.

glib is already most of the way there. gspawn's posix_spawn() fast path handles
the ordinary case, and on a system without fork() it is not a fast path but the
only path. What is left is to stop referring to fork() when it does not exist:

  - meson checks for it, the same way it already checks for posix_spawn()
  - the gspawn fallback sets G_SPAWN_ERROR_FORK and says which condition sent
    it there, rather than being a link-time failure or a late crash
  - the intermediate-child fork is unreachable once the first one is gone
  - g_on_error_stack_trace() and g_on_error_query() report ENOSYS. Attaching a
    debugger to yourself needs a second process; these are developer aids, and
    saying so beats not linking.
  - g_test_trap_fork(), deprecated since 2.38 in favour of the re-executing
    g_test_trap_subprocess(), fails the way a failed fork() already fails there:
    fatally, because a test that believed it forked and did not would run the
    child's assertions in the parent.
  - the GTestDBus watchdog takes its existing -1 arm, which warns. It exists to
    make a leaked dbus-daemon die with the test process, and that genuinely
    needs a second process outliving this one.

And one thing that is not about fork() at all, but is the same shape of
problem -- a capability the compiler reports and the hardware does not have:
G_ATOMIC_LOCK_FREE is forced off on ARM M-profile. LDREX/STREX there have no
exclusive monitor covering the memory this runs from, so an inline atomic does
not terminate. cc.links() cannot see it, because the test compiles and links
and would only fail by spinning, and meson does not run it when cross-
compiling. With it off, GLib emulates its atomics with a mutex, which works
because the C library's mutexes are implemented with a kernel compare-exchange.

That alone is not enough, and the reason is worth stating: USE_NATIVE_MUTEX
builds GMutex directly out of __atomic builtins and a futex, bypassing
g_atomic_* and so bypassing the setting above. It is enabled whenever futex and
atomics are *present*, never asking whether they are lock-free -- and building a
mutex out of emulated atomics, which are themselves implemented with a mutex, is
circular even where the hardware works. It now also requires G_ATOMIC_LOCK_FREE.
Those three functions were the last six exclusive instructions in the library.

Nothing changes on a system that has fork(), or working exclusives.

Note that the posix_spawn() path needs a libc whose implementation works
without fork() -- uClibc's returns ENOSYS on NOMMU whenever file actions are
passed, which glib always does. See br-external/patches/uclibc/0001.

Upstream status: not submitted.

--- a/meson.build
+++ b/meson.build
@@ -874,6 +874,14 @@
   glib_conf.set('HAVE_POSIX_SPAWN', 1)
 endif
 
+# fork() is not universal. A NOMMU target cannot have it -- without address
+# translation the copy would share the parent's memory -- so uClibc does not
+# provide the symbol there at all, and referring to it is a link error rather
+# than something that shows up when the code runs.
+if cc.has_function('fork', prefix : '#include <unistd.h>')
+  glib_conf.set('HAVE_FORK', 1)
+endif
+
 # Check whether strerror_r returns char *
 if have_func_strerror_r
   if cc.compiles('''#define _GNU_SOURCE
@@ -2074,8 +2082,33 @@
 #endif
 '''
 
+# Cortex-M: the compiler has atomic ops, and they do not work.
+#
+# LDREX/STREX need an exclusive monitor, and on the M-profile part this is being
+# built for there is none covering the memory the system runs from -- external
+# memory behind a QSPI interface. STREX there never succeeds. An inline atomic
+# is therefore not merely slow, it does not terminate: the compare-exchange
+# retry loop spins forever on its first use.
+#
+# cc.links() cannot detect that. The test program compiles and links perfectly
+# well; it would only fail by never returning, and meson does not run it when
+# cross-compiling. So ask the architecture instead of the linker.
+#
+# With this false, GLib emulates its atomics with a mutex (gatomic.c). That is
+# correct here: the C library's mutexes on this target are implemented with a
+# kernel-assisted compare-exchange rather than with exclusives, so they work
+# where the inline ops do not.
+arm_m_profile_test = '''
+#if !defined(__arm__) || defined(__ARM_ARCH_ISA_ARM)
+#error not M-profile
+#endif
+int main (void) { return 0; }
+'''
+
 # We know that we can always use real ("lock free") atomic operations with MSVC
-if cc.get_id() == 'msvc' or cc.get_id() == 'clang-cl' or cc.links(atomictest, name : 'atomic ops')
+if cc.compiles(arm_m_profile_test, name : 'ARM M-profile without usable exclusives')
+  have_atomic_lock_free = false
+elif cc.get_id() == 'msvc' or cc.get_id() == 'clang-cl' or cc.links(atomictest, name : 'atomic ops')
   have_atomic_lock_free = true
   if cc.get_id() == 'gcc' and not cc.compiles(atomicdefine, name : 'atomic ops define')
     # Old gcc release may provide
